            <ArticleTitle>The Surface of Ice Is Like Supercooled Liquid Water.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>15540-15544</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/anie.201707530</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The surface of ice has been reported to be disordered at temperatures well below the bulk melting point. However, the precise nature of this disorder has been a topic of intense debate. Herein, we study the molecular properties of the surface of ice as a function of temperatures using heterodyne-detected sum-frequency generation spectroscopy. We observe that, down to 245 K, the spectral response of the surface of ice contains a component that is indistinguishable from supercooled liquid water.</AbstractText>
